CBOOT: the bootstrap C bundle of patsopt
========================================

The compiler is written in ATS2. To build it without any ATS compiler, the C code that a working patsopt
generates for every module is distributed as a bundle in this directory. Nothing here is kept in git except this
README, Makefile, mkboot.sh, stripc.py and .gitignore: the bundle is generated and is part of release tarballs.

Generating (needs a working patsopt and python3; see ../README)

    make -C src CBOOT [PATSOPT=...]       # runs mkboot.sh

mkboot.sh generates, for C3NSTRINTKND=gmpknd and intknd, the C code of every module (pats_*_dats.c, pats_error_sats.c, the
main module with and without main() for libatsopt, libatsopt_ext_dats.c) and writes:

    *.c               the C code that does not depend on the integer kind (the time stamps and all the comments of the generated code are removed by stripc.py)
    gmpknd/*.c intknd/*.c   the modules that differ (pats_intinf, pats_lintprgm_myint, pats_constraint3_solve)
    pats_runtime_ats1.c, libatsopt_ext.c   hand-written C of src/
    inc/              the headers that the C code includes: ccomp/runtime, prelude/CATS, libats/CATS, src/*.cats
    modules.mk        the lists of the files (included by Makefile)

The generated *_gen*.hats files (arena encodings) are already expanded in the C code: python3 is not needed to use the bundle.

Using (a C compiler, make and libgmp; nothing else)

    make -C src/CBOOT -j8                  # ./patsopt (needs gmp)
    make -C src/CBOOT KIND=intknd          # ./patsopt without gmp
    make -C src/CBOOT libatsopt.a          # the library version (the API of utils/libatsopt)
    make -C src/CBOOT CC=clang CFLAGS=-O1 LDFLAGS=...

Then build the real compiler from the sources with it (stage 2; the bundle's patsopt is stage 1, compiled from the C that
the compiler that generated the bundle produced):

    make -C src PATSOPT=$PWD/src/CBOOT/patsopt         # PATSHOME = this repository
    # or: make -f Makefile_devl PATSOPT=$PWD/src/CBOOT/patsopt all

If the compiler that generated the bundle is itself a stage-2 build, the C of the bundle is the C that the normal build
compiles: the patsopt of the bundle and the one of `make -C src` are then the same program up to the path strings in the
generated C (comments and assertion messages hold the paths of the tree that generated the bundle).
